    "result_text": "I added the three password reset helpers to `CTFd/utils/security/email.py`. I didn't run or test them. The pattern check reports no high-severity deviations.\n\n- **`generate_password_reset_token(addr, timeout=1800)`** creates a random 256-bit token. It stores the email in the cache under `reset_password_<token>` for 30 minutes and returns the token.\n- **`verify_password_reset_token(nonce)`** returns the email for the token. It raises `UserConfirmTokenInvalidException` if the token is unknown or expired.\n- **`remove_password_reset_token(nonce)`** deletes the token, so it can't be used again. Call it after the password has been changed.\n\nI built these from the catalog pattern `security.email_confirm_token@v1`, which the existing email-confirm helpers in that file also follow. The only change from the pattern is the cache key prefix. That keeps reset tokens separate from email-confirmation tokens, so one kind can't be used as the other. The `# pcat:` markers are kept above each function.\n\nMy first draft raised `UserResetPasswordTokenInvalidException`, which the file already imports. The pattern check flagged that as a high-severity deviation from the pattern's exception. Your request didn't ask for a different exception, so I switched to `UserConfirmTokenInvalidException`.\n\nIf your reset flow needs to catch the reset-specific exception, tell me. That change needs a `pcat-allow` line, and a human has to confirm it in the pull request. The file still imports `UserResetPasswordTokenInvalidException`, which nothing in it uses."
